# Google Flights Deals through HasData

One tool, `hasdata_google_travel_flights_deals_getGoogleFlightsDeals`. It needs `q`, a free-text trip description, and `departureId`, the origin as a three-letter uppercase IATA code. Everything else is optional, and most of it comes with a condition.

This is the inspiration end of flight search. The user says "cherry blossom in Japan" and Google picks both the destinations and the dates. For a trip somebody has already decided on, the plain Google Flights tool is the right call instead.

## Every filter needs `arrivalId`

`type`, `travelClass`, `outboundDate`, `returnDate`, `travelDuration`, `tripLength`, `stops`, `maxDuration`, `maxPrice`, `includeAirlines` and `excludeAirlines` all require a pinned destination. Sending any of them without `arrivalId` returns 422 with `rule: "requiredIfExists"` and costs nothing, so the mistake surfaces immediately rather than hiding in a plausible-looking answer.

So a request for direct flights under a price cap while letting Google choose the destination is asking for two incompatible things, and the API refuses it rather than guessing. Pin a destination or drop the filters, and say which you did.

Three of them also exclude each other. `returnDate`, `travelDuration` and `tripLength` are three ways to say how long the trip is, and only one can be set. `includeAirlines` and `excludeAirlines` are the same way round.

## Fields that are present on some deals and not others

`discountPercent` arrives only when Google considers the fare discounted. Across 351 measured deals it was on 109, and where it was absent the fare still sat 4% to 15% under `typicalPrice`. `typicalPrice` was on every deal, so compute the comparison when the field is missing. An absent key is not a zero discount.

`airline` and `airlineCode` disappear together when the itinerary is flown by more than one carrier, and `multipleAirlines` is true instead. The split is exact: in 351 measured deals all 82 that set the flag omitted both fields and all 269 that did not carried both. Read the flag before naming a carrier, and say "several airlines" rather than reporting a blank.

## Read the dates back before quoting a price

`searchInformation.dateRange` is the window Google actually searched, and for a seasonal query it can be months out. A fare found for next March is not a fare for next month, so quote the range alongside the price rather than presenting the number alone.

`searchInformation` also reports the `departure` airport in full, the `priceRange` found and the `airlines` involved, which is the cheapest way to sanity-check that the query was read the way the user meant.

## Reading a deal

A deal carries `outboundDate` and `returnDate`, `price` and `typicalPrice` as `{ value, currency }` pairs, `durationMinutes`, `tripLengthDays`, `stops`, the `departureAirport` and `arrivalAirport`, a `destination` block with the city, country and a short highlight, and `bookingLink`, a direct Google Flights link.

`durationMinutes` is per flight rather than for the whole trip. Convert it before showing it to a reader, because 1320 means twenty-two hours.

Each successful call spends credits from the connected account, and one origin per call means comparing two cities costs two calls.
